From c4eea29cfae8db6d0bb1a91e01ee57d9acc141f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 05:19:30 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 47/62] render: blend modes, compose layers and composite FBOs
 the way Wallpaper Engine does

Decoded from wallpaper64.exe 2.8.485 (GH = the headless Ghidra decompile):
- A pass that samples the FBO it draws into (genericimage3's BLENDMODE g_Texture4, a compose layer's
  _rt_FullFrameBuffer on its last pass) reads a copy taken just before the draw. WE copies
  _rt_FullFrameBuffer before the image or the first pass whose material reads it (GH 0x140214b50,
  once per frame for particles, GH 0x14025bc30); in GL that read was a feedback loop (undefined).
  Only for a sampler the program uses: genericimage4/genericparticle declare g_Texture3 =
  _rt_MipMappedFrameBuffer for REFLECTION/REFRACT, compiled out otherwise. CWallpaper::copyForSampling
  keeps one copy per size and flags.
- colorBlendMode 31 (A + B*opacity) is additive blending of the layer's last draw, with no BLENDMODE
  pass (the combo is 0 for modes 0 and 31, GH 0x140211f90; draw blend 2, GH 0x140214b50/0x1402321d0).
- Image flags at +0x304 (constructor default 0x8040, GH 0x140212c50): copybackground 0x40,
  nointerpolation 0x4000, clampuvs 0x8000, all parsed now. The composite FBOs clamp per clampuvs
  (default true) and sample nearest per nointerpolation or the texture (GH 0x140214b50); they took the
  texture's flags, so solid and most layers wrapped.
- A passthrough (compose) layer renders its subtree into itself (GH 0x140218260 / 0x1402181b0, up to a
  nested compose layer), after copying the background (copybackground) or clearing to transparent
  (GH 0x1402321d0); its effects then apply to both, and with copybackground false its last draw is
  translucent. Here: a scene-sized group target its base pass reads; the subtree's last passes, text
  and particles draw into it and the scene loop skips them. With no effect running the target is
  blitted back (composelayer's base pass covers its whole destination; WE never draws it to the screen).
- The composite ping-pong starts by the parity of the passes that write it, so the last composite
  written is _rt_imageLayerComposite_<id>_a (what the last pass reads) and _b the stage before
  (GH 0x140214b50, start index = pass count parity); another layer binding them gets that stage.
- The scene clear writes alpha (the last pass left it masked), so the scene's alpha is opaque.

Origin: ours
Why: docs/player/spikes/we-lwe-gap-audit.md B7 (E27, E28, E30/G33, E31, E33)
